cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "MESH: connectome-scale contact detection and EM proofreading for putative gap junctions"
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below. The version
  accompanying the paper is archived separately at
  https://github.com/ActiveSensing/MESH — cite that one for the published
  analysis; this repository is the ongoing development version.
type: software
# TODO before submission: confirm the author list, add ORCIDs and co-authors.
authors:
  - family-names: Serbe
    given-names: Etienne
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ETigerschuss/MESH"
abstract: >-
  MESH detects physical appositions between neurons reconstructed in the FlyWire
  connectome of the FAFB electron-microscopy volume, and supports their manual
  validation in EM. Contacts are found geometrically (k-d tree over mesh
  vertices, 100 nm proximity), grouped into discrete sites, confined to genuine
  membrane apposition by segmentation adjacency, and rendered as EM image stacks
  in an interactive viewer where an expert can measure, accept or reject each
  site. Because chemical synapses are annotated in FlyWire but gap junctions are
  not, subtracting annotated chemical synapses from validated appositions yields
  a ranked list of putative electrical-synapse sites. This development build
  additionally contains a biophysical circuit model of the LPTC-motor-neuron
  network (Hodgkin-Huxley cells coupled by gap junctions and chemical synapses)
  and an eye-movement readout.
keywords:
  - connectomics
  - Drosophila
  - FlyWire
  - FAFB
  - electron microscopy
  - gap junctions
  - electrical synapses
  - biophysical modelling
license: MIT
